2025-05-01 03:05:50 - Thread-1187 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and how they will be used  
player.player_id           : joins to every other table that stores yearly statistics for players  
player.name_given          : the “given name” (first & middle) that needs to be returned  
batting.player_id          : joins to player table  
batting.g                  : regular‑season Games Played (to locate the highest single‑season total)  
batting.r                  : Runs scored (highest single‑season total)  
batting.h                  : Hits (highest single‑season total)  
batting.hr                 : Home Runs (highest single‑season total)  

Typical filters / conditions we will apply  
• WHERE batting.g   IS NOT NULL  
• WHERE batting.r   IS NOT NULL  
• WHERE batting.h   IS NOT NULL  
• WHERE batting.hr  IS NOT NULL  
• Find the MAX value of each of those four columns, then return the player(s) and value(s) that match the MAX.  

Below are 10 exploratory SQL queries (all capped at 20 rows) that move from simple inspection to the final answer.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at a few batting rows to see the basic columns we care about
SELECT player_id, year, stint, g, r, h, hr
FROM batting
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Distinct non‑null values of Games Played, highest first
SELECT DISTINCT g
FROM batting
WHERE g IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY g DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. The single highest Games Played value in the data set
SELECT MAX(g) AS max_games
FROM batting;
```

```sql
-- 4. Which player(s) posted that max Games Played season?
SELECT b.player_id, b.year, b.g
FROM batting AS b
WHERE b.g = (SELECT MAX(g) FROM batting)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Player(s) with the most Runs scored in one season
SELECT b.player_id, b.year, b.r
FROM batting AS b
WHERE b.r = (SELECT MAX(r) FROM batting)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Player(s) with the most Hits in one season
SELECT b.player_id, b.year, b.h
FROM batting AS b
WHERE b.h = (SELECT MAX(h) FROM batting)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Player(s) with the most Home Runs in one season
SELECT b.player_id, b.year, b.hr
FROM batting AS b
WHERE b.hr = (SELECT MAX(hr) FROM batting)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Join each of the four max tables to PLAYER to show the given names
SELECT p.name_given, b.g AS max_games
FROM batting AS b
JOIN player AS p ON p.player_id = b.player_id
WHERE b.g = (SELECT MAX(g) FROM batting)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. One query per category with the given names (Runs example)
SELECT p.name_given, b.r AS max_runs
FROM batting AS b
JOIN player  AS p ON p.player_id = b.player_id
WHERE b.r = (SELECT MAX(r) FROM batting)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final combined answer: the given names and record values for all four categories
SELECT DISTINCT
       CASE WHEN b.g  = (SELECT MAX(g)  FROM batting) THEN p.name_given END   AS games_record_holder,
       b.g  AS games_value,
       CASE WHEN b.r  = (SELECT MAX(r)  FROM batting) THEN p.name_given END   AS runs_record_holder,
       b.r  AS runs_value,
       CASE WHEN b.h  = (SELECT MAX(h)  FROM batting) THEN p.name_given END   AS hits_record_holder,
       b.h  AS hits_value,
       CASE WHEN b.hr = (SELECT MAX(hr) FROM batting) THEN p.name_given END   AS hr_record_holder,
       b.hr AS hr_value
FROM batting AS b
JOIN player  AS p ON p.player_id = b.player_id
WHERE b.g  = (SELECT MAX(g)  FROM batting)
   OR b.r  = (SELECT MAX(r)  FROM batting)
   OR b.h  = (SELECT MAX(h)  FROM batting)
   OR b.hr = (SELECT MAX(hr) FROM batting)
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries walk through the data set step‑by‑step, ultimately identifying the players (by their “given name”) who own the single‑season records for Games Played, Runs, Hits, and Home Runs, along with the corresponding record numbers.
